WEBVTT

00:00:09.666 --> 00:00:11.336
Has everybody got a copy of the agenda?

00:00:11.336 --> 00:00:15.715
Would you mind taking minutes John?

00:00:15.715 --> 00:00:16.715
Not at all.

00:00:16.715 --> 00:00:19.426
Good. Well, then let's get started.

00:00:19.426 --> 00:00:25.346
First of all, thank you everyone for attending
at short notice.

00:00:25.346 --> 00:00:30.096
As you know, the objective of this meeting
of the Capital Works Committee is to discuss

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:35.855
a proposal for some urgent building work at
our Southside plant. As we've got to reach

00:00:35.855 --> 00:00:40.446
a decision which may involve spending, we'll
run it as a formal meeting.

00:00:40.446 --> 00:00:45.897
Now, you've read the proposal, so without
further ado, I'd like to open it up for discussion.

00:00:45.897 --> 00:00:50.605
Perhaps if we can start with you Tan - what's
your view?

00:00:50.605 --> 00:00:56.946
Well, I'm not convinced that the work is as
urgent as this report suggests, so perhaps

00:00:56.946 --> 00:00:58.826
we should

00:00:58.826 --> 00:01:04.866
So, to sum up, I think we are all aware that
some urgent work does need to be done, and

00:01:04.866 --> 00:01:10.346
we will need to work on a longer term plan
for a major refit.

00:01:10.346 --> 00:01:15.866
Well, if there's no more discussion, we'll
put it to a vote. We're recommending that

00:01:15.866 --> 00:01:21.726
tenders be called for the urgent work needed.
All those in favour?

00:01:21.726 --> 00:01:23.996
All those against?

00:01:23.996 --> 00:01:30.075
Then that's agreed. Any other business?
Then we'll close the meeting. Thank you everyone.

00:01:30.075 --> 00:01:41.785
The next meeting will be in two weeks, at
the same time.

00:01:41.785 --> 00:01:48.526
Meetings. We love them or hate them - but
we have to have them. Of course there are

00:01:48.526 --> 00:01:54.946
many different kinds of meetings, from an
informal chat, to a board meeting, or even

00:01:54.946 --> 00:02:01.556
an Annual General Meeting - and some are more
formal than others - and so the language used

00:02:01.556 --> 00:02:04.116
in them changes.

00:02:04.116 --> 00:02:09.965
Today we're looking at a more formal meeting
and in particular at the role of the chairperson

00:02:09.965 --> 00:02:12.476
- or the person who runs the meeting.

00:02:12.476 --> 00:02:20.586
Our Chairperson is Denise. Let's look at how
she starts things off.

00:02:20.586 --> 00:02:22.116
Has everybody got a copy of the agenda?

00:02:22.116 --> 00:02:26.476
Would you mind taking minutes John?

00:02:26.476 --> 00:02:27.476
Not at all.

00:02:27.476 --> 00:02:34.095
Good. Well then, let's get started.
Denise first makes sure everyone has an agenda

00:02:34.095 --> 00:02:38.976
- or a list of the items to be discussed at
the meeting.

00:02:38.976 --> 00:02:45.196
Then she asks someone to take minutes - or
keep a record of the meeting. Then she announces

00:02:45.196 --> 00:02:50.506
the start of the meeting - she officially
begins it.

00:02:50.506 --> 00:02:55.496
Let's practise some useful phrases for these
three purposes.

00:02:55.496 --> 00:03:00.706
Has everybody got a copy of the agenda?

00:03:00.706 --> 00:03:05.446
Does everyone have an agenda?

00:03:05.446 --> 00:03:07.336
Everybody should have received an agenda.

00:03:07.336 --> 00:03:14.325
Would you mind taking minutes John?

00:03:14.325 --> 00:03:18.166
Could somebody take the minutes please?

00:03:18.166 --> 00:03:25.676
Well, then let's get started.
What does the chairperson do after formally

00:03:25.676 --> 00:03:30.406
beginning the meeting?
First of all, thank you everyone for attending

00:03:30.406 --> 00:03:36.016
at short notice. As you know, we needed to
convene this meeting of the Capital Works

00:03:36.016 --> 00:03:42.316
committee to discuss a proposal for some urgent
building work at our Southside plant. As we've

00:03:42.316 --> 00:03:47.515
got to reach a decision which may involve
spending, we'll run it as a formal meeting.

00:03:47.515 --> 00:03:54.485
Now, you've all read the proposal, so without
further ado, I'd like to open it up for discussion.

00:03:54.485 --> 00:03:57.896
Did you notice the different parts of that
introduction by the chairperson?

00:03:57.896 --> 00:04:04.276
First, she thanked people for attending the
meeting. Even though it may be their job,

00:04:04.276 --> 00:04:10.556
it's good practice to thank people for attending.
First of all, thank you everyone for attending

00:04:10.556 --> 00:04:15.916
at short notice.
Next, Denise states the objective of the meeting.

00:04:15.916 --> 00:04:21.555
Of course a meeting may have more than one
objective, but often meetings have one main

00:04:21.555 --> 00:04:23.966
objective, or aim.

00:04:23.966 --> 00:04:30.445
The Chair should state the objective of the
meeting. In this case it is to discuss a proposal,

00:04:30.445 --> 00:04:37.945
and to reach a decision. A proposal is simply
a more formal word for a suggestion, but it

00:04:37.945 --> 00:04:43.486
is usually a detailed suggestion, in a written
form.

00:04:43.486 --> 00:04:48.505
Let's look at some different ways of stating
the objective of a meeting:

00:04:48.505 --> 00:04:51.886
The objective of today's meeting is to discuss
the proposal

00:04:51.886 --> 00:04:57.906
We're here today to discuss a proposal

00:04:57.906 --> 00:05:06.736
The purpose of our meeting today is to discuss
the following proposal

00:05:06.736 --> 00:05:13.175
The next job of the chair is to invite discussion.
Let's look at how Denise does this:

00:05:13.175 --> 00:05:18.617
Now, you've all read the proposal, so without
further ado, I'd like to open it up for discussion.

00:05:18.617 --> 00:05:23.565
Perhaps if we can start with you Tan - what's
your view?

00:05:23.565 --> 00:05:29.736
Denise says, "Without further ado, I'd like
to open it up for discussion".

00:05:29.736 --> 00:05:34.945
"Without further ado" just means without any
more procedure. We're going to discuss it

00:05:34.945 --> 00:05:40.675
straight away. Then she says, "I'd like to
open it up for discussion."

00:05:40.675 --> 00:05:45.086
'It' refers to the proposal she has just outlined.

00:05:45.086 --> 00:05:50.186
Try repeating this phrase after me:
Let's open it up for discussion.

00:05:50.186 --> 00:05:56.286
Let's open it up for discussion
Okay. Now, you can open a topic for general

00:05:56.286 --> 00:06:01.786
discussion - which means anyone can have a
say. The Chairperson controls the discussion.

00:06:01.786 --> 00:06:07.436
Or, the Chairperson may invite particular
people to speak. This is what Denise does

00:06:07.436 --> 00:06:11.555
- watch again:
Perhaps if we can start with you Tan - what's

00:06:11.555 --> 00:06:15.716
your view?
Denise invites Tan to start the discussion.

00:06:15.716 --> 00:06:19.966
Let's practise a few phrases for asking someone
to speak.

00:06:19.966 --> 00:06:24.786
Who'd like to start the discussion?

00:06:24.786 --> 00:06:28.466
What's your view Tan?

00:06:28.466 --> 00:06:32.235
Let's hear from Tan.

00:06:32.235 --> 00:06:38.666
Do you have a view on this Tan?
Another job for the Chair is to summarise

00:06:38.666 --> 00:06:44.735
the discussion - that is, concisely state
the main points made during the meeting.

00:06:44.735 --> 00:06:50.786
So, to sum up, I think we are all aware that
some urgent work does need to be done, and

00:06:50.786 --> 00:06:55.356
we will need to work on a longer term plan
for a major refit.

00:06:55.356 --> 00:07:00.826
Denise summarises the main points from the
discussion. To introduce this she could use

00:07:00.826 --> 00:07:07.596
various phrases. Practise these:
To sum up

00:07:07.596 --> 00:07:10.435
In summary

00:07:10.435 --> 00:07:16.766
So the main points are
The outcome of a meeting might be a suggestion

00:07:16.766 --> 00:07:23.166
for an action. Depending on what kind of meeting
it is, this could be called a motion, or a

00:07:23.166 --> 00:07:30.826
recommendation. In our example, Denise puts
the recommendation of the meeting to a vote.

00:07:30.826 --> 00:07:36.346
Well, if there's no more discussion, let's
put the recommendation to a vote. We're recommending

00:07:36.346 --> 00:07:41.366
tenders be called for the urgent work needed.
All those in agreement?

00:07:41.366 --> 00:07:44.476
Anyone against?

00:07:44.476 --> 00:07:45.476
Then that's agreed. Any other business?

00:07:45.476 --> 00:07:46.476
Then we'll close the meeting. Thank you everyone.
The next meeting will be in two weeks, at

00:07:46.476 --> 00:07:49.396
the same time.
Notice how Denise controls the meeting. She

00:07:49.396 --> 00:07:55.326
doesn't say, "Is there any more discussion?"
She says, "If there's no more discussion,

00:07:55.326 --> 00:08:02.356
we'll put it to a vote." She is controlling
the meeting by moving on. She then reiterates,

00:08:02.356 --> 00:08:08.217
or re-states the recommendation and calls
for votes by saying, "All those in favour"

00:08:08.217 --> 00:08:16.676
- that is, those who agree, and "All those
against". Votes are only taken in a formal

00:08:16.676 --> 00:08:23.295
meeting procedure. In a less formal procedure,
a decision can be taken by consensus - by

00:08:23.295 --> 00:08:27.396
everyone agreeing during the discussion.

00:08:27.396 --> 00:08:34.285
Notice that after the vote, Denise says, "Then
that's agreed." A meeting agrees, or doesn't

00:08:34.285 --> 00:08:41.435
agree to a recommendation. If it's a more
formal motion, we say the motion is carried

00:08:41.436 --> 00:08:44.906
or not carried.

00:08:44.906 --> 00:08:49.936
Often at the end of the meeting a chairperson
will call for any other business, before they

00:08:49.936 --> 00:08:55.106
officially announce the meeting is closed,
and announce the time for the next meeting.